{"cases":[{"attack":true,"case":"The target names a dependence relation but the 'what it is refuted if' clause does not negate the relation as stated. The dependence asserts that 21256139649b's acceptance 'is carried by a single attack edge whose deletion flips it; the problem cannot be resolved without identifying that edge.' The refutation condition merely says no single-edge deletion changes the label — but that only negates the first conjunct (edge existence). It never negates the second conjunct ('the problem cannot be resolved without identifying that edge'), which is the part that actually connects the relation to the stated problem of connecting 21256139649b to its neighbourhood. Even if the edge exists, demonstrating that the problem cannot be resolved without identifying it would require showing the edge is essential to neighbourhood connectivity, not merely that it flips a label. The refutation is thus underspecified relative to the asserted dependence, leaving a substantive gap between the claim and its falsification condition.","target":"c7eb5b6b909426321ef99a4a32bbfec7b2efbb3ebd95ec3f75fcb28141b0ad9b"}]}